  "query_preview": "/SUNDAY THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 238 AM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nOn Sunday, ensemble models are in strong agreement that a weak\nridge would likely start to flatten out over the area\ntransitioning into zonal flow aloft. After this, we can expect\nthe upper-level pattern to change again on Monday morning into \nsouthwesterly flow as a trough system is forecast to form and \nbegin working its way over from the Four Corners region. On \nTuesday and onward, as the trough starts moving to the east with\na possible ridge in tow, uncertainty within the guidance starts\nto grow and shows two possible scenarios. The first Scenario is\nfor the trough to amplify to the southeast into the Oklahoma \nand Arkansas area as it slides past as the ridge to the west \nstarts to weaken. The second scenario is for the trough to \namplify to south much sooner and slides through Texas as the \nridge amplifies into the middle Rockies and splitting off to the\nnortheast. However with the growing uncertainty, ensemble \nguidance is favoring the first scenario to occur. With that \nbeing said the pattern then looks to move in a northwesterly \nflow Wednesday afternoon. Winds looks to slightly pick up Sunday\nand Monday afternoon with speeds around 15 mph while clouds \ncoverage increase Monday and remain throughout the period. \n\nTemperatures between Sunday and Tuesday are forecast to have\nslight cooling trend. Afternoon Highs are forecast to drop from\nthe 40s on Sunday to the 30s Tuesday. Morning lows are expected\nto follow a similar, but more gradual trend as temperatures \nwould likely stay in the 20s with a few areas in northern Yuma \ncounty possibly reaching down into the upper 10s. Temperatures \non Wednesday look to rise back up with highs in the 40s again. \n\nWhile there is no precipitation to be expected on Sunday and\nmost of Monday, GEFS and LREF ensemble guidance are hinting an\nincreased potential for winter weather than prior model runs\nfrom yesterday on Monday night and Tuesday. We can expect a weak\ncold front to pass through and moisture from the south to allow\nrain/snow mix to occur within west of KS-25 Monday afternoon. As\nthe temperature drops overnight, precipitation is expected to \nturn into snow as it moves eastward throughout the entire \nforecast area. However, as temperatures rises above freezing \nlater in the day on Tuesday, we can then expect precipitation \nto revert back rain/snow mix. With the increased potential in \nthe forecast guidance, there is low confidence (25%) that \nwintery precipitation to occur with no expected snow \naccumulation at this time.",
